---
import {
  DOCKER_DOCS_URL,
  GITHUB_SERVER_RELEASES_URL,
  LSIO_REPO_URL,
  PERMISSIONS_DOCS_URL,
  SYNOLOGY_DOCS_URL,
  UNRAID_DOCS_URL,
} from '@/data/links'
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro'

const compose = `services:
  audiosilo:
    image: ghcr.io/kodestar/audiosilo-server:latest
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"
    volumes:
      - ./data:/data            # db, config, certs (first run prints admin creds once)
      - /srv/audiobooks:/library:ro
    environment:
      PUID: "1000"              # owner of ./data (Unraid: 99, Synology: usually 1026)
      PGID: "1000"              # group of ./data (Unraid: 100, Synology: usually 100)
      AUDIOSILO_WEB_DIR: /app/web
      # AUDIOSILO_PUBLIC_URL: "https://books.example.com"  # used in QR/invite links`

const up = `mkdir -p data              # create the data folder first
docker compose up -d
docker compose logs        # first run prints the admin password + auth code ONCE`

const binary = `# Choose the admin password in your browser instead of reading it from the log:
./audiosilo --setup --data ./data

# Or run headless - it auto-creates an admin and prints the password + auth code once:
AUDIOSILO_TLS_MODE=off ./audiosilo --data ./data`

const update = `docker compose pull && docker compose up -d`
---
<DocsLayout
  title="Installation"
  description="Install AudioSilo Server with Docker (recommended), on Unraid from Community Applications, or as a native binary."
>
  <p>
    AudioSilo Server is the only thing you install. Every option below includes the web player, so
    the apps work out of the box. <strong>Docker is the recommended path</strong>
    for always-on, NAS, VPS and multi-user setups.
  </p>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Docker</strong> (to run the published image), or a single downloaded binary.</li>
    <li>A folder of audiobooks (m4b, or mp3 parts - the scanner figures out the shape).</li>
    <li>
      <strong>ffmpeg/ffprobe</strong> is optional but recommended (durations and chapters), so the Docker
      image includes it; the native binaries fetch it automatically on first run if it isn't present.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Option 1: Docker (recommended)</h2>
  <p>
    Make a folder for AudioSilo with a <code>docker-compose.yml</code> in it, mounting your audiobooks
    read-only and persisting <code>/data</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename="docker-compose.yml" code={compose} />
  <p>
    Create the <code>data</code> folder before the first start, then start it and read the logs to grab
    your one-time admin credentials:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={up} />
  <p>
    Set <code>PUID</code>/<code>PGID</code> to the user and group that own the data folder. The server
    runs as that user, and it must be able to read and write the folder.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Linux: your <code>id -u</code>/<code>id -g</code>.</li>
    <li>Unraid: <code>99</code>/<code>100</code>.</li>
    <li>
      A NAS such as Synology: the folder's owner, usually your NAS user (<code>1026</code>/<code>100</code>
      on Synology). Give that user read and write on the folder in the NAS's file manager. See the
      <a href={SYNOLOGY_DOCS_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Synology install guide</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Backups go in <code>data/backups</code>. A backup folder mounted elsewhere
    (<code>AUDIOSILO_BACKUP_DIR</code>) needs the same owner. If
    the container keeps restarting or says permission denied, see
    <a href={PERMISSIONS_DOCS_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">permission problems</a> in the
    troubleshooting guide. The <a href={DOCKER_DOCS_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Docker install
    guide</a> has the full walkthrough.
  </p>
  <p>Updating the server or the bundled player is always a new image:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={update} />

  <h2>Option 2: Unraid</h2>
  <p>
    On Unraid, install from <strong>Community Applications</strong>: search the Apps tab for
    <code>AudioSilo</code>. The app is the
    <a href={LSIO_REPO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinuxServer.io</a> image,
    <code>lscr.io/linuxserver/audiosilo</code>, with the web player and ffmpeg inside, rebuilt for every
    server release.
  </p>
  <p>
    Point <strong>Appdata</strong> (<code>/config</code>) at a cache or pool path such as
    <code>/mnt/cache/appdata/audiosilo</code>, and <strong>Audiobooks</strong> (<code>/audiobooks</code>) at
    your books share. Leave <code>PUID</code>/<code>PGID</code> at <code>99</code>/<code>100</code>. On
    first start the admin password and auth code are printed once in the container log (in the Docker
    tab, click the AudioSilo icon, then Logs). In the admin console, add <code>/audiobooks</code> as your
    library: the path inside the container, not your <code>/mnt/user</code> share. The
    <a href={UNRAID_DOCS_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unraid install guide</a> covers the template
    fields, reverse proxies and moving from the image above.
  </p>

  <h2>Option 3: Native binary</h2>
  <p>
    Download the archive for your OS/arch from the
    <a href={GITHUB_SERVER_RELEASES_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">server releases page</a>
    (<code>.tar.gz</code>, <code>.zip</code>, or a <code>.deb</code>/<code>.rpm</code> that installs a systemd
    service), extract it, and run:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={binary} />
  <p>
    The <code>--data</code> directory holds the config, database and certs. The web player is embedded
    in the binary, so <code>/web</code> just works.
  </p>

  <h2>After it's running</h2>
  <p>
    By default the server listens on <code>:8080</code> with a self-signed certificate. Open
    <code>https://localhost:8080/admin</code> to reach the admin console, or continue with the
    <a href="/docs/first-run">first-run guide</a> to add a library and pair a device. To put it on the
    internet safely, see <a href="/docs/reverse-proxy">reverse proxy &amp; TLS</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
